    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i know about zotero, but sometimes even the software feels like it's failing me. like, i'll fix a specific citation, but then i'll look back a page later and there's another one that's just... slightly off. it's almost like these little errors are actively working against me, hiding in the text and popping up just to mess with my sense of order. it's driving me crazy how these small, irritating things just seem to multiply and clutter up my work. do you think there's a psychological reason why these tiny mistakes feel so much more intrusive than a big error?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It sounds like you're experiencing the cognitive load of perfectionism, which is a very common phenomenon when dealing with highly structured tasks like academic writing. These small errors feel more intrusive because they violate the *pattern* you are trying to establish, and the human brain is wired to notice deviations from expected order. It's like a small, persistent noise in a quiet room—it demands attention far more than a sudden, loud crash.\n\nTo combat this, instead of trying to fix everything at once, try a targeted review pass. Dedicate one session solely to checking author names and dates, ignoring everything else. This compartmentalization helps prevent the entire document from feeling like a chaotic mess, which can be overwhelming. You're fighting a battle against tiny, insidious details, and treating it like a massive siege is counterproductive.",
      "source": "target",
